# Line endings.
#
# Git on Windows checks out with CRLF by default (core.autocrlf=true). Shell
# scripts in this repository are executed INSIDE Linux containers - the Kafka
# image runs create-topics.sh and make-truststore.sh from a bind mount - and
# bash does not tolerate a trailing \r:
#
#     scripts/foo.sh: line 2: $'\r': command not found
#     scripts/foo.sh: line 18: syntax error: unexpected end of file
#
# The working copies happen to be LF today, so this file is not fixing a break;
# it is preventing one that a FRESH CLONE on Windows would produce. That matters
# here specifically: reproducibility by a reader is a claim this project makes
# (docs/generator-spec.md 7), and a reader who clones and runs `run.ps1 up`
# would meet the failure, not the author.
#
# Learned from IBM/AMLSim on 2026-08-31, whose six shell scripts arrived as CRLF
# from a Windows clone and failed exactly this way inside a container.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
docker-compose.yml text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the Windows-side entry point and stays native.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Data files are compared by SHA-256 in docs/generator-spec.md. Any rewriting of
# line endings would change the hash and silently break the "dataset of record".
*.csv           -text
